{"graph_chars": "Society faces the same logic Alphonso does. The production possibilities frontier, or P P F, shows the combinations of two goods an economy can produce with limited resources. The book's example is healthcare versus education. Point A puts every resource into healthcare and none into education. Point F does the reverse. Points on the curve are possible and use resources fully. Points inside are possible but wasteful. Points outside are not possible today. The slope of the P P F is the opportunity cost of one more unit of the good on the horizontal axis. Two differences from Alphonso's budget line. First, the budget constraint is a straight line because relative prices, from the consumer's point of view, are fixed. The P P F is curved because of the law of diminishing returns, also called the law of increasing opportunity cost: as you produce more of a good, the marginal opportunity cost of producing it rises. Resources are not equally good at everything. Pull the last doctors into classrooms and you lose a lot of health for a little education. Second, the P P F often has no specific numbers on the axes, because we are illustrating a principle, not a census. Growth, more labor and capital, or better technology, shifts the whole frontier outward. Until then, more of one good means less of another.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0624], [0.0624, 0.1248], [0.1248, 0.1872], [0.1872, 0.2496], [0.2496, 0.312], [0.312, 0.3745], [0.3745, 0.4369], [0.4369, 0.4993], [0.4993, 0.5617], [0.5617, 0.6241], [0.6241, 0.6865], [0.6865, 0.7489], [0.7489, 0.8113], [0.8113, 0.8737], [0.8737, 0.9361], [0.9361, 0.9985], [0.9985, 1.0609], [1.0609, 1.1234], [1.1234, 1.1858], [1.1858, 1.2482], [1.2482, 1.3106], [1.3106, 1.373], [1.373, 1.4354], [1.4354, 1.4978], [1.4978, 1.5602], [1.5602, 1.6226], [1.6226, 1.685], [1.685, 1.7474], [1.7474, 1.8099], [1.8099, 1.8723], [1.8723, 1.9347], [1.9347, 1.9971], [1.9971, 2.0595], [2.0595, 2.1219], [2.1219, 2.1843], [2.1843, 2.2467], [2.2467, 